From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: LONGEVITY:  How long can we live now?

I am very interested in Life Extension.  A lot of Extropian-types seem to 
be predicting cryonic-restoration of life by the year 2100.  I hope that I 
am still alive by then!  I was born in 1963.  If I live to be 137 (the same 
age as Dr. McCoy when he visited Picard's Enterprize) it will be the year 
2100!  I will see the 22nd Century!  I think it is a much simpler problem 
to keep my body alive to the age of 137, then the cryonics approach.

I believe that many of us alive today will never die.  I think that the 
solutions to uploading or rejuvinating the body will be solved before we 
have to resort to cryonics!  (I am a member of Alcor, just in case of 
accidents, and because the premiums are cheaper if you buy younger.)

Any ideas on how to measure your true biological age?  Most of the theories 
I have read (I have a biology minor) don't seem to conclusively correspond 
with aging and death.  We can improve appearance and the function of 
specific systems of the body.  Antioxident damage seems to be the only 
global measure of aging, and it is very hard to measure.

By the way, at age 30, I still get carded to see if I am 21 (for alcohol).  
I have a twin brother who people confused with me when we were younger, but 
now we have grown to look totally different.  I like to think this means 
that my anti-aging program is working, but I have no objective 
measurements.
