Subject: Tim May's Social Punctuated Equilibriumism

At 12:50 AM 12/22/93 +0000, Ray wrote:
>I just think that "strong statements" will add a little noise to
>the soup of the list because many people will read them without
>taking them in context, especially new comers. How would one feel about
>the list (presuming one joined because of a reading of the welcome
>message) and one's first received post makes "strong statements" about welfare
>ghetto trash? Not that I disagree with Tim's statements but it could lead
>people to the wrong ideas and the anti-Extropian forces will certainly
>latch onto the opportunity as they have done with Libertarians.

The list is presently trying to fulfill two conflicting roles: that of a
"safe haven" for already-Extropians to exchange strategies, ideas, and
news; and that of an outreach vehicle.  This is the fundamental problem
with the S/N ration, the flame wars, and the "use of strong language".

The ExI-BATF (Bay Area Task Force) is currently writing a FAQ for
alt.extropians.  Once that's done, we're planning to post it on a regular
basis and do "newbie wrangling" on that newsgroup, sifting the wheat from
the chaff by inviting those with potential to join the private mailing
list. The list itself can then go back to being a much simpler thing
without a lot of legalistic bullshit, because only the "hard corps" will be
present.

Outreach through a newsgroup rather than through a private list will
require significantly greater diplomatic skills -- I don't expect most
folks from the list to participate.  It will be a challenge to one's
patience and composure to answer the same questions week in and week out on
alt.extropians.

The other reason for activating the alt. group is to serve as a precedent
for having a talk. group later.  As things stand now, if we tried to create
a "mainstream" newsgroup, the criticism could be raised, "There's already
an alt. group, and no one's using it."  So after a few months of using the
alt. group, it should be possible to switch to a talk. group that'll go
just about everywhere.
