From: plaz@netcom.com (Geoff Dale)
Subject: PPL: A proposal

Harry writes:
>a conscious being, KPJ Jaakkola wrote:
>> The PPL-1 feels too much like a centralized State bureaucracy for my 
>> liking.
>> The Harvey Newstrom decentralized PPL appeals more to me.
>
>I agree. There are very critical differences. Anyone can leave the
>list (and our PPL) while it is hard to "leave the state." In others
>you presence on the list is not forced.
Extropia, love it or leave it.

Am I right in assuming that any new PPL has to maintain the PPL-1 rules as
a minimum? If this is true, there is a serious flaw in the "if you don't
like it, create your own" argument. Strangely, you are no longer using this
argument. It used to be a standard refrain.

People seem to want a less restrictive PPL.

If allowed, I would like to create a PPL that eliminates the anti-flaming,
and anti-basics rules. Call it PPL-A (for anarchy). I still would "outlaw"
spoofing and any other form of fraud including libel and slander,
forwarding list messages to a third party (defined as a party not
subscribed to the list) without the author(s) permission. In addition, if a
formal complaint is made on a person for excessive and repeated rudeness by
a member of my PPL or any other PPL, I will consider ejecting or banning
that person on a case-by-case basis, but never before cautioning the person
that he is treading the line and giving him the chance to change his tune.

I think I can live with the mandatory META, FORWARD/FWD, POLL, and PLEDGE
prefixes, but I can't imagine actually punishing anyone for it unless they
persisted after many warnings.

Public plonkings, setting the facts straight, and defending one's honor
will explicitly be permitted. These, as a matter of fact, happen all the
time in PPL-1 with no one batting an eye. Judiciously used, these can act
as a powerful social feedback mechanism.

On top of this, I commit to do what a PPL is supposed to do, protect its
members. I will protect them from arbitrary judgments of other PPLs and the
tempers of list members. I will go to bat for them as long as they stay
within my PPL's rules. The ExI-Board still has the right to expel a person
from the list, but I promise to be my clients' advocate in that forum as
well. (Not that I claim to be a Director, but that I will argue with the
directors in your behalf, again as long you stay within PPL-A's rules.)

If making a less strict PPL is verboten, I call (again) for a basic change
in the list rules.

As a note, I still think that ad hominem attacks, me-too messages, and
utter cluelessness (aka newbie basics discussions) are bad things. I just
don't "think there ought to be a law". People can protect themselves from
this. This fine list software we now have especially empowers people to
::exclude idiots, and people who offend them.

If you are interested in joining PPL-A, send private e-mail to me at
plaz@netcom.com, and cc: Harry Shapiro <habs@panix.com>, so that we can
tell how many people are behind this concept. I'll probably repost after
the holiday season is over to allow people who aren't receiving the list at
the moment, to participate in this decision making process.

