Date: 28 Dec 93 18:18:39 EST
From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Subject: Question on Great Genetics Debate

Phil Fraering writes: <A) A lot of you seem to be arguing that genes are atomic.
Is this the case? Or are y'all simply hoping they act as if they are?  B) How do
the people on this list feel about group selection?>

A) I'm not sure what you mean by atomic, perhaps genes acting singly and
deterministically instead of within a complex genomic regulatory system?  I vote
for complex interactions where tweaking genes always entails risk because of
unpredictability of results.  There is an epistemological barrier to knowing how
changes in systems as complex as this will ultimately turn out other than
statistical descriptions of intended consequences.  The basic unit of
evolutionary selection for humans is the self, not atomic genes.

B) This argument between individual selection and group selection is one the
hottest topics in science right now.  While I respect Edelman's Theory of
Neuronal Group Selection as a major step in our understanding of the physiology
of consciousness, I have to land on the side of individual selection for
everything from people to neurons to bugs to antibodies.  I believe that
evidence that points towards group selection no matter where it shows up is
being misinterpreted.  I think Kauffman is onto the correct interpretation when
he lays out complex attractors as universal biological rules.  These attractors
tend to pull individuals towards a common area in genotypic and phenotypic space
thus giving the appearence of group selection but it's still individual.
Natural selection is a weaker force than self-oganization in biological systems.

The value of free advice is beyond worth.

