Date: Tue, 28 Dec 93 16:30:38 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Book Review: _Grizzly_Years_

Peacock, Doug, 1990.  _Grizzly_Years_.  Henry Holt and Company, Inc.
  Paperback edition, 1992.  Kensington.

    I recommend a "nature" book here because the author possesses many
Extropian qualities, notably including the meta-virtue of being
willing to examine his beliefs and act upon what he discovers,
notwithstanding contrary social and legal proscriptions.  Naturalist
and former new-lefty Doug Peacock returned from horrendous Vietnam
duty as a Green Beret combat medic, as a severe case of "walking
wounded", and sought the preservation of his soul in wilderness.
During the next two decades he became increasingly involved with the
field study of the subspecies of _Ursus_horribilis_ known as the
grizzly bear -- for the most part while alone, unarmed, on the ground,
and at ranges occasionally down to one or two meters.
    I bought the paperback of _Grizzly_Years_ from curiosity prompted
by the grizzly/entomologist encounter I described here recently.  I
was not disappointed.  Peacock is a superb field ethologist, well
acquainted with the communicative behavior of the baddest non-human
animal in the lower forty-eight; he relates numerous instances (such
as on p. 85) in which he successfully used the bears' own repertoire
of postural and facial signals to negotiate mutual withdrawal from a
life-threatening situation.  It is no exaggeration and only slightly
metaphoric to say that when he visits bear country, he speaks the
language of the natives well enough to get along.
    Peacock has also been involved in the politics of wildlife
"management" and wilderness preservation, and in this context exhibits
other qualities that Extropians might find interesting.  He certainly
doesn't like government.  But he doesn't like business and
entrepreneurial activity either, at least insofar as it involves
messing with wilderness.  As a matter of fact, he doesn't seem to like
anybody very much, excepting bears.  Back-country conflicts between
wildlife enthusiasts and everybody else have often considerably
transcended the political and attained sabotage and violence, up to
and including the destruction of vehicles and aircraft.  Peacock has
been involved.  For a sampler, try pp. 47-8, in which he blows away
a telephone booth with a shotgun.
    I've joked here about "the right to arm bears"; but if you think
of Doug Peacock as a mercenary in their service, he comes across as
quite Extropian.
                                          -- Jay Freeman

